  "unit": "u07-directions",
  "title": "Near, far, next to",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "English puts 'near, behind, next to' *before* the noun; Bengali puts them **after** it, as **postpositions**. The noun takes the genitive 'of' ending first:\n\n- **স্টেশনের কাছে** — 'near the station' (lit. 'station's near')\n- **বাড়ির পাশে** — 'next to the house'\n- **হোটেলের সামনে** — 'in front of the hotel'\n\nRemember: **-এর** after a consonant (স্টেশন → স্টেশনের), **-র** after a vowel (বাড়ি → বাড়ির, বাসা → বাসার).\n\nMany of these words also work alone: **বাবা বাইরে** 'Dad is outside', **আপু উপরে** 'Apu is upstairs'. All of them end in **-এ** — they are really locative nouns ('at the near-side', 'at the front').",
